<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Once upon a Time... – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1em; color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Legends of Panama</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Once upon a Time...</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify main ideas and supporting details in spoken texts. Interactive: Ensure mutual understanding in group interactions through clarification and confirmation.</p>
            <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify key elements in spoken legends (e.g., "Who is the hero in the story?"). Can clarify and respond to questions about the order of events to support group interactions (e.g., "What happened first?"). Can summarize a story's events during discussions (e.g., "The hero saved the village").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of famous Panamanian landmarks (e.g., Volcán Barú, Gatún Lake, beaches) and some fantasy images (e.g., a mythical creature, a brave warrior, an ancient village). The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and then, "Do you know any stories about these places or characters?" Students listen to the questions and share any prior knowledge in short phrases.</p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary words for the lesson: <i>legend, hero, monster, village, fight, save, brave</i>. The teacher says each word clearly and slowly, modeling correct pronunciation, especially focusing on inflectional endings like in "was fighting" and past simple "saved" or "celebrated". The teacher writes "Once upon a time..." on the board and explains it as a common beginning for stories, giving an example: "Once upon a time, a brave hero lived in a small village." Students listen and repeat the new vocabulary chorally.</p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'legend' a true story or an old, traditional story?" (Expected answer: An old, traditional story.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone is 'brave', are they scared or not scared?" (Expected answer: Not scared.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we say at the beginning of a story? 'Hello' or 'Once upon a time'?" (Expected answer: Once upon a time.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a simplified Panamanian legend about a brave hero saving a village from a monster. The legend incorporates target vocabulary (<i>legend, hero, monster, village, fight, save, brave, long ago, suddenly</i>) and grammar (past simple, past continuous).</p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understand main ideas and some details in short, simple spoken narratives.</p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio recording once. Students listen for the general idea.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> After the first listening, the teacher asks: "What kind of story is this? Is it a funny story or a legend?" and "Who are the main characters in the story?" (Expected answers: A legend. A hero and a monster.) Students listen and respond.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen for specific details and complete a True/False activity based on the story. Example statements: "Long ago, a monster was living in a big city." (False) "A brave hero saved the village." (True)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Past simple (narrating events: <i>celebrated, ended</i>), Past continuous (actions in progress: <i>was fighting, was exploring</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: <i>legend, hero, monster, village, fight, save, rescue, brave, once upon a time, suddenly, long ago</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: Practice pronunciation of inflectional endings -ed, -ing; phonemic awareness of -ed sounds /t/, /d/, or /ɪd/ (e.g., 'saved' /d/, 'fought' /t/, 'waited' /ɪd/).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled practice (Listen and Complete):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences from the legend, but with key words or verb forms missing. Students listen to short audio clips (sentence by sentence) from the legend and fill in the blanks. E.g., "Long ago, a terrible _______ lived near the _______." (Expected: monster, village). "The hero _______ (fight) the monster when it _______ (appear) suddenly." (Expected: was fighting, appeared). Teacher monitors and provides feedback on listening accuracy and verb forms.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled (Sequencing Events - Listen and Order):</b> Students receive shuffled sentences describing events from the legend. The teacher plays specific parts of the audio again. Students listen carefully to the order of events and number the sentences correctly (e.g., "What happened first? The hero explored the mountain or the monster attacked the village?"). They focus on time markers like "once upon a time," "suddenly," "after," "before."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Peer Check and Clarification):</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from the sequencing activity. They take turns reading one of their ordered sentences and their partner listens. If there's a disagreement or confusion, they must clarify by asking questions like: "What did you hear for number one?" "Can you repeat what happened after that?" or "Did you say 'the hero saved' or 'the hero was saving'?" This encourages active listening and mutual understanding.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Group Story Re-telling and Clarification</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher assigns each group a part of the legend (beginning, middle, or end).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the full audio legend one last time. Students listen to the complete story to refresh their memory.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses and prepares to orally re-tell their assigned part of the legend to the class. They must use the target vocabulary (<i>legend, hero, monster, village, fight, save, brave</i>), grammar (past simple, past continuous), and sequencing words (<i>once upon a time, suddenly, long ago</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When presenting, one student from each group starts. Other groups listen carefully. If something is unclear or they miss a detail, a listening group can interrupt to ask for clarification (e.g., "Sorry, what was the hero doing at the beginning?", "Did the monster appear suddenly or slowly?"). This emphasizes sociolinguistic competence of clarifying details in group discussions. The presenting group must respond and clarify.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Formative assessment:</b> Teacher observation during group re-telling, focusing on active listening and comprehension questions.</p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays a short, new, very simple audio clip (2-3 sentences) of a different Panamanian legend. Students listen and then individually answer two comprehension questions orally or in writing on an exit ticket, such as: "Who was the main character in this short story?" and "What was the main character doing?"</p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify main characters/actions from spoken text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., hero, monster, village).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key words and verb endings (-ed, -ing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of responses when clarifying details during group interaction.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word you learned today about legends?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to understand the legend today? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did listening carefully help you understand the story better today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to legends do you want to practice more?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The class engaged well with the audio and practiced clarifying. The controlled practice for past simple/continuous was effective. Some students still struggle with distinguishing -ed endings and sequencing events. More targeted listening practice for sequence markers will be needed. Peer clarification helped but needs more direct instruction on specific phrases (e.g., "Could you say that again?").</p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Next lesson, we will read a written version of a Panamanian legend. We will see many of the same words we heard today and explore how they look on the page. We will also practice identifying details in a written text."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>